1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,260
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,720
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,560
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,400
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:20,540
Today, our topic is one task at a time.

8
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:23,300
One thing, then the next thing.

9
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,560
Ben, how long is your list today?

10
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:28,280
Hi Mia.

11
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:28,860
Hi everyone.

12
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:32,060
My list today has fourteen things.

13
00:00:33,260 --> 00:00:33,760
Emails.

14
00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:35,300
A call to the bank.

15
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:36,840
A broken lamp.

16
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:41,860
Fourteen things, and so far I have done only two.

17
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,520
And I looked at the list for a long time before I started.

18
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:48,580
What about you, Mia?

19
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:50,320
How long is your list?

20
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:53,180
Today, only nine, Ben.

21
00:00:53,980 --> 00:00:55,500
Nine is a good day for me.

22
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:58,920
But last Thursday, it was twenty-three.

23
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:02,040
Twenty-three things, and one day.

24
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:03,600
Emails.

25
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:04,540
A report.

26
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:05,760
Call the bank.

27
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:07,000
Buy a gift.

28
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:08,400
Fix the printer.

29
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:11,460
I looked at the list, and I did nothing.

30
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:13,460
For one hour.

31
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:16,840
One hour of nothing, Mia?

32
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:18,570
With twenty-three things waiting?

33
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:20,580
I know that hour.

34
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:26,240
You sit, you look, and the list looks back at you.

35
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:27,179
What did it feel like?

36
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:29,760
One hour, Ben.

37
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:33,250
I looked, and I felt heavy, and I did nothing.

38
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:36,399
Twenty-three things is not a list.

39
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:37,490
It is a wall.

40
00:01:38,430 --> 00:01:40,540
And I think many people know that wall.

41
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:44,400
You look at everything, and you cannot start anything.

42
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:46,320
A wall, Mia.

43
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:47,200
Yes.

44
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:49,580
I know that wall very well.

45
00:01:50,610 --> 00:01:53,370
And today, we talk about how to get over it.

46
00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:55,580
So here is our plan.

47
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:59,580
In part one, we talk about our own lists.

48
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:02,280
What happens when the list is long.

49
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:07,620
In part two, we ask why one task at a time works.

50
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:10,600
And I have a true story for that.

51
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:15,820
In part three, we share simple ideas for the next heavy day.

52
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:18,860
Does that sound good?

53
00:02:18,870 --> 00:02:19,880
That sounds good, Ben.

54
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:24,760
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

55
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:26,460
You will hear it again.

56
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:31,480
You can listen while you walk, while you cook, or while you look at your own long list.

57
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:33,220
Okay, Ben.

58
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:34,079
Part one.

59
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:38,820
Do you want to hear what happened after my hour of nothing?

60
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:40,160
Of course, Mia.

61
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:43,140
I have been waiting for this all week.

62
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:45,540
How did you start, in the end?

63
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:47,280
Did the wall go away by itself?

64
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:49,180
Or did somebody help?

65
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:51,540
My friend called, Ben.

66
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:55,820
She asked, "What are you doing?" I said, "Nothing.

67
00:02:56,230 --> 00:03:00,820
I have twenty-three things." And she said something simple.

68
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:08,180
She said, "What is the first one?" I said, "Reply to Anna's email." She said, "Do that.

69
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:09,700
Then call me back."

70
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:15,650
"Then call me back." Mia, that is a good friend.

71
00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:19,109
She did not ask about the twenty-three things.

72
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:21,210
She asked about one.

73
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:24,520
So you did the one email?

74
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:24,991
Just that one?

75
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:27,540
I did, Ben.

76
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:29,030
One email.

77
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:30,280
Five minutes.

78
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:31,800
Then I called her back.

79
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:36,320
And she said, "What is the next one?" And we did this all morning.

80
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:37,480
One task.

81
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:38,660
Then the next.

82
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:42,000
By lunch, I had finished eleven things.

83
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:42,600
Eleven.

84
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:46,650
In one morning, after one hour of nothing.

85
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:47,740
Eleven things, Mia.

86
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:50,399
That is a very good morning.

87
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:52,320
And the question was so simple.

88
00:03:52,350 --> 00:03:58,220
"What is the first one?" Not, "How will I do all of this?" That question makes the wall.

89
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:02,440
But "What is the first one?" That question makes a door.

90
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:04,260
A door, Ben.

91
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:05,000
Yes.

92
00:04:05,540 --> 00:04:07,500
One email is not a wall.

93
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:10,200
One email is a thing with edges.

94
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:12,620
I can see the start and the end.

95
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:14,020
Five minutes.

96
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:16,120
What about you?

97
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:17,860
What do you do when your list is a wall?

98
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:20,020
I have one question too, Mia.

99
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:25,580
I ask, "What can I finish in ten minutes?" Not the most important thing.

100
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:27,040
Not the hardest thing.

101
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,840
The thing I can finish in ten minutes.

102
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:31,900
Reply to an email.

103
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:33,460
Pay one bill.

104
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:36,290
Because finishing is the medicine.

105
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:38,520
Finishing is the medicine?

106
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:40,940
That is a strange sentence, Ben.

107
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:44,740
I thought starting was the hard part, not finishing.

108
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:45,360
What do you mean?

109
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:49,940
When I finish one small thing, something in my head changes, Mia.

110
00:04:50,790 --> 00:04:52,580
The wall becomes a little smaller.

111
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:54,640
And I want to finish another one.

112
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:57,250
Finishing makes the next one easier.

113
00:04:57,980 --> 00:04:59,540
So I start with the easy finish.

114
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:00,690
Ten minutes.

115
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:02,420
Done.

116
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:03,880
Then the next one.

117
00:05:03,890 --> 00:05:08,420
"Finishing makes the next one easier." Ben, I felt that on Thursday.

118
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:11,940
After the first email, there was a small click.

119
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:12,310
Done.

120
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:14,820
And the click made me want the next click.

121
00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:16,880
I think that is the secret.

122
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:18,180
Not the task.

123
00:05:18,580 --> 00:05:19,100
The click.

124
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:21,160
The click, Mia.

125
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:21,800
Yes.

126
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:24,160
Twenty-three things give no click.

127
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:25,880
They are never finished.

128
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:29,220
But one email gives a click in five minutes.

129
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:33,740
And eleven clicks in a morning is a very good morning.

130
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:36,240
Can I tell you about a task I carried for four months?

131
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:38,560
Four months, Ben?

132
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:40,760
That is a long time to carry one task.

133
00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:42,200
Was it a big task?

134
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:43,200
A report?

135
00:05:43,460 --> 00:05:44,660
A project?

136
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:45,580
What kind of task?

137
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:47,880
A form, Mia.

138
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:50,920
One form for the city, for my car.

139
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:52,849
It needed twenty minutes.

140
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:55,820
And I looked at it every day for four months.

141
00:05:56,620 --> 00:05:58,820
Every day, twenty seconds of guilt.

142
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:02,600
Twenty seconds, times one hundred and twenty days.

143
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:06,160
That is forty minutes of guilt for a twenty-minute task.

144
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:09,770
Forty minutes of guilt for twenty minutes of work.

145
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:12,300
Ben, that is a bad trade.

146
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:14,560
And I think I have ten forms like that in my life.

147
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:16,340
What happened to the form?

148
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:17,680
Did you do it in the end?

149
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:20,080
One Tuesday, I did it, Mia.

150
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:21,540
Twenty minutes.

151
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:22,569
Click.

152
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:24,280
And the guilt was gone.

153
00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:29,700
So now, when a small task knocks for the third time, I do it next.

154
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:31,720
Not because it is important.

155
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:34,610
Because carrying it costs more than finishing it.

156
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:41,840
"Carrying it costs more than finishing it." Ben, I have a form like that right now, actually.

157
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:42,780
Okay.

158
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:45,580
That is my next task, after this recording.

159
00:06:46,130 --> 00:06:48,100
Let me say it out loud so it is real.

160
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:50,280
Next, the form.

161
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:51,600
Twenty minutes.

162
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:52,420
Click.

163
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:54,260
Next, the form.

164
00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:55,620
Good, Mia.

165
00:06:55,660 --> 00:06:56,680
We will ask you next time.

166
00:06:57,500 --> 00:06:59,260
And what about the other twenty-two things?

167
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:01,980
While you do one email, do they go away?

168
00:07:02,920 --> 00:07:03,879
No, Ben.

169
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:05,000
They wait outside the door.

170
00:07:05,520 --> 00:07:06,180
But they knock.

171
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:10,420
While I am doing one thing, my head says, "What about the report?

172
00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:14,420
What about the bank?" And the knocking pulls me out of the one thing.

173
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:16,660
Do you know that knocking?

174
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:18,300
Every day, Mia.

175
00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:20,560
So here is what I do.

176
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:22,280
I write the knocking down.

177
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:23,920
On a paper next to me.

178
00:07:23,970 --> 00:07:24,430
"Report.

179
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:28,540
Bank." And I say to the paper, "You are next.

180
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,410
Wait." And my head is quiet, because the thing is written.

181
00:07:32,740 --> 00:07:33,290
It is safe.

182
00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:35,380
It will not be forgotten.

183
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:36,840
Write the knocking down.

184
00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:38,440
That is smart, Ben.

185
00:07:39,030 --> 00:07:42,800
The paper holds the twenty-two, so my head can hold the one.

186
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:45,200
I do something similar with my phone.

187
00:07:45,860 --> 00:07:47,200
Phone in another room.

188
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:51,000
Because the phone is twenty-two more things, knocking.

189
00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:53,280
Every message is a task.

190
00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:55,640
Every message is a task.

191
00:07:55,660 --> 00:07:56,760
Yes, Mia.

192
00:07:56,900 --> 00:08:01,760
So, paper for the knocking, another room for the phone, and one task on the table.

193
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:04,120
Okay, so that was part one.

194
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:05,120
Our lists.

195
00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:13,200
Twenty-three things, one hour of nothing, a good friend, eleven clicks, and a form that waited four months.

196
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:15,100
Now, part two.

197
00:08:15,820 --> 00:08:17,540
Why does one task at a time work?

198
00:08:18,250 --> 00:08:20,750
Mia, do you want to start, or should I tell my story?

199
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:22,960
Tell your story, Ben.

200
00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:26,430
You said it is a true story, and you said it is small.

201
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:27,960
I like small true stories.

202
00:08:28,290 --> 00:08:30,300
They stay in my head longer than big ones.

203
00:08:31,100 --> 00:08:31,940
Okay, part two.

204
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:34,560
This is a true story, Mia.

205
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:37,780
It is a small story from a book, and I think about it every week.

206
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:42,140
The book is "Bird by Bird," by an American writer, Anne Lamott.

207
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:44,460
It came out in 1994.

208
00:08:45,220 --> 00:08:48,220
She tells a story about her brother, when he was ten years old.

209
00:08:48,860 --> 00:08:49,760
Ten years old?

210
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:52,000
Ben, I remember being ten.

211
00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:54,040
Everything was big at ten.

212
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:56,220
A school report was a mountain.

213
00:08:56,780 --> 00:08:57,700
What happened to him?

214
00:08:58,440 --> 00:09:00,400
He had a school report about birds, Mia.

215
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:02,420
He had three months to do it.

216
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:04,540
And he did nothing for three months.

217
00:09:05,240 --> 00:09:09,500
Then, the night before the report was due, he sat at the kitchen table.

218
00:09:09,560 --> 00:09:09,950
Crying.

219
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:13,300
Oh, I know that table, Ben.

220
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:14,980
I have sat at that table.

221
00:09:15,020 --> 00:09:17,460
Books all around, and nothing on the paper.

222
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:19,320
We all have, Mia.

223
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,240
He had books about birds all around him.

224
00:09:22,280 --> 00:09:23,080
Paper.

225
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:23,650
Pencils.

226
00:09:24,220 --> 00:09:26,250
And a whole report to write in one night.

227
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:27,840
He could not start.

228
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:29,160
It was too big.

229
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:31,800
And then his father sat down next to him.

230
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:33,720
He put his arm around the boy.

231
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:36,620
And he said, "Bird by bird, buddy.

232
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:38,680
Just take it bird by bird."

233
00:09:39,390 --> 00:09:42,360
"Bird by bird." Ben, I love that.

234
00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:43,840
One bird.

235
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:45,560
Write about one bird.

236
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:47,180
Then the next bird.

237
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:51,180
And the father did not say, "You should have started three months ago."

238
00:09:51,740 --> 00:09:53,720
That was true, Mia, but it did not help.

239
00:09:54,300 --> 00:10:00,520
He did not say, "Work faster." He said, "One bird." He made the wall into a bird.

240
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:02,820
And a boy can write about one bird.

241
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:05,320
That is the whole story.

242
00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:10,310
And Anne Lamott made it the title of her book, because it is the best advice she ever heard about work.

243
00:10:11,010 --> 00:10:12,900
The best advice about work, Ben.

244
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:15,520
And I think I know why it works.

245
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:17,920
Our heads cannot hold twenty-three things.

246
00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:18,460
Not really.

247
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:21,160
We can hold one thing, maybe two.

248
00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:25,100
When we look at twenty-three, the head does not see tasks.

249
00:10:25,380 --> 00:10:26,360
It sees a cloud.

250
00:10:27,080 --> 00:10:27,660
Is that right?

251
00:10:28,220 --> 00:10:29,040
That is right, Mia.

252
00:10:29,550 --> 00:10:30,940
And nobody can start a cloud.

253
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:33,920
One bird is a thing with edges.

254
00:10:34,020 --> 00:10:36,560
One email is a thing with edges.

255
00:10:36,660 --> 00:10:38,900
So the trick is not to work harder.

256
00:10:38,940 --> 00:10:41,020
The trick is to look at one thing with edges.

257
00:10:41,580 --> 00:10:43,100
And then the next thing with edges.

258
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:47,640
"Nobody can start a cloud." Ben, can I ask about time?

259
00:10:48,240 --> 00:10:51,000
Because on Thursday, I noticed something strange about time.

260
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:52,600
Strange how, Mia?

261
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:53,860
Time is time.

262
00:10:54,640 --> 00:10:58,100
Twenty-three things or one thing, the day has the same hours.

263
00:10:58,760 --> 00:10:59,540
What did you notice?

264
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:03,840
When I looked at twenty-three things, I felt I had no time, Ben.

265
00:11:04,340 --> 00:11:05,240
Not one minute.

266
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:08,220
But when I looked at one email, I had time.

267
00:11:08,660 --> 00:11:09,340
Five minutes.

268
00:11:09,730 --> 00:11:10,200
Plenty.

269
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:12,400
Time did not change.

270
00:11:12,460 --> 00:11:13,740
The day was the same length.

271
00:11:14,380 --> 00:11:17,280
But twenty-three things ate all my time before I started.

272
00:11:17,860 --> 00:11:20,500
And one task took only its own five minutes.

273
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:22,340
"A cloud eats time.

274
00:11:22,920 --> 00:11:26,280
A task uses time." Mia, that is exactly it.

275
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:28,660
And I make a mistake with time, too.

276
00:11:29,440 --> 00:11:32,660
I think, "I have eight hours, and twenty-three things.

277
00:11:33,140 --> 00:11:37,300
That is twenty minutes each." And then I try to do everything in twenty minutes.

278
00:11:37,860 --> 00:11:39,100
And nothing gets finished.

279
00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:41,480
I am not doing tasks.

280
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:42,600
I am watching a clock.

281
00:11:43,420 --> 00:11:45,060
Watching a clock, Ben.

282
00:11:45,100 --> 00:11:48,559
And the clock makes you fast, and fast makes you bad.

283
00:11:48,680 --> 00:11:50,540
So what is the good way to think about time?

284
00:11:51,380 --> 00:11:53,420
One task, and the time it needs, Mia.

285
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:55,840
Not the time I want to give it.

286
00:11:55,880 --> 00:11:56,749
The time it needs.

287
00:11:57,529 --> 00:11:59,540
The email needs five minutes.

288
00:11:59,580 --> 00:12:00,980
The report needs two hours.

289
00:12:01,640 --> 00:12:02,560
I do not mix them.

290
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:06,060
The time belongs to the task, not to the clock.

291
00:12:06,940 --> 00:12:10,290
And when I work this way, I am slower, but I finish more.

292
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:13,180
Slower, but you finish more.

293
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:17,040
Because finished things do not come back, Ben.

294
00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:21,040
Half-finished things come back tomorrow, and knock.

295
00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:25,480
My half-finished things are the loudest knockers.

296
00:12:26,260 --> 00:12:28,080
The report I started and stopped.

297
00:12:28,710 --> 00:12:30,600
The email I opened and closed.

298
00:12:32,260 --> 00:12:33,660
The loudest knockers, Mia.

299
00:12:33,980 --> 00:12:34,320
Yes.

300
00:12:34,920 --> 00:12:39,800
So maybe the rule is, do not start the next one until this one is finished.

301
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:42,080
One bird, fully written.

302
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:43,579
Then the next bird.

303
00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:47,100
Not five birds, half-written, all crying.

304
00:12:48,220 --> 00:12:53,480
"Not five birds, half-written, all crying." Ben, I want to put that on my wall.

305
00:12:54,580 --> 00:12:56,020
Okay, so that was part two.

306
00:12:56,500 --> 00:12:58,640
Why one task at a time works.

307
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:00,860
We talked about the boy and his father.

308
00:13:01,409 --> 00:13:04,080
We said the head cannot hold twenty-three things.

309
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:09,170
And we said a cloud eats time, and a task uses time.

310
00:13:10,020 --> 00:13:11,020
Now, part three.

311
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:14,100
Simple ideas for the next heavy day.

312
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:15,940
Ben, you go first.

313
00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:17,820
Okay, part three.

314
00:13:18,620 --> 00:13:20,820
My first idea is for big tasks, Mia.

315
00:13:21,440 --> 00:13:25,080
Some people are thinking, "But Ben, the report is two hours.

316
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:28,220
I cannot finish it in ten minutes." Yes.

317
00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:32,220
So for big tasks, I make a small first task.

318
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:42,460
Not "write the report." But "write the first page." Or "write the title and three lines." That is a bird.

319
00:13:42,470 --> 00:13:43,520
That can be finished.

320
00:13:44,580 --> 00:13:47,000
A thing with one page finished, Ben.

321
00:13:47,620 --> 00:13:50,300
That is the swimsuit from our last episode.

322
00:13:50,380 --> 00:13:51,840
Make the thing ready.

323
00:13:52,850 --> 00:13:54,510
My first idea is a question.

324
00:13:55,120 --> 00:13:59,410
When a new task comes while I am working, I ask, "Is it on fire?"

325
00:14:00,470 --> 00:14:03,520
"Is it on fire?" Mia, I like that.

326
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:07,640
Because every new task feels like fire when it arrives.

327
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:11,720
The message, the call, the small problem.

328
00:14:11,820 --> 00:14:13,920
And is it on fire?

329
00:14:14,040 --> 00:14:14,370
Usually?

330
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:17,080
Almost never, Ben.

331
00:14:17,120 --> 00:14:18,900
It feels on fire.

332
00:14:18,960 --> 00:14:20,409
It knocks like fire.

333
00:14:21,009 --> 00:14:24,320
But most things can wait twenty minutes while I finish one email.

334
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:26,380
Real fires are rare.

335
00:14:26,840 --> 00:14:27,760
Maybe one a month.

336
00:14:28,580 --> 00:14:30,160
Everything else goes on the paper.

337
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:31,280
It waits.

338
00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:33,400
I finish my bird first.

339
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:35,980
Real fires are rare, Mia.

340
00:14:36,509 --> 00:14:39,560
And sometimes the "fire" is not even there anymore.

341
00:14:40,060 --> 00:14:41,080
It solved itself.

342
00:14:41,900 --> 00:14:44,120
I learned that on my worst day.

343
00:14:44,240 --> 00:14:48,080
A deadline, a sick child, a broken car.

344
00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:54,260
I tried to do it all at once, and by six o'clock, everything was half-finished, all crying.

345
00:14:55,040 --> 00:14:56,700
Now, one at a time.

346
00:14:57,800 --> 00:15:00,560
My second idea is about the moment between tasks.

347
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:02,240
I finish one thing.

348
00:15:02,920 --> 00:15:03,200
Click.

349
00:15:03,840 --> 00:15:05,580
And then there is a small hole.

350
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:07,039
Ten seconds.

351
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:08,680
A hole?

352
00:15:09,620 --> 00:15:10,750
Ben, I know that hole.

353
00:15:11,260 --> 00:15:15,120
Ten seconds after a click, and then the day can go two ways.

354
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:16,910
What happens in your hole?

355
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:21,260
In the hole, my hand goes to the phone, Mia.

356
00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:25,780
Or my eyes go to the whole list, and the cloud comes back.

357
00:15:26,660 --> 00:15:29,980
So now, before the hole, I decide the next task.

358
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:36,729
When I finish one thing, I say, out loud, "Next: the bank." One sentence.

359
00:15:37,460 --> 00:15:40,280
And I start the next task before the hole opens.

360
00:15:41,080 --> 00:15:45,320
"Next: the bank." Ben, I do that in a different way.

361
00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:48,160
I keep the next task next to me.

362
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:53,720
If the next task is the bank, the bank letter is on my desk, next to my hand.

363
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:56,920
When I finish the email, my hand finds the letter.

364
00:15:57,380 --> 00:15:58,440
I do not need to think.

365
00:15:58,860 --> 00:16:00,740
The next thing is already here.

366
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:04,320
The next thing is already here.

367
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:05,320
That is smart, Mia.

368
00:16:06,160 --> 00:16:11,160
Then the hole between tasks has something in it, and the cloud has no room.

369
00:16:11,180 --> 00:16:12,190
Do you have a third idea?

370
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:14,880
Something for the evening, maybe?

371
00:16:15,020 --> 00:16:15,680
Yes, Ben.

372
00:16:16,060 --> 00:16:18,520
My third idea is for the evening.

373
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:20,540
At the end of the day, I look at my list.

374
00:16:21,020 --> 00:16:22,300
Some things are finished.

375
00:16:22,570 --> 00:16:23,490
Some are not.

376
00:16:24,240 --> 00:16:28,360
In the past, I looked at the unfinished things, and I felt bad.

377
00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:30,880
Now I choose one thing for tomorrow morning.

378
00:16:31,620 --> 00:16:31,960
One.

379
00:16:32,490 --> 00:16:33,700
The first bird.

380
00:16:34,400 --> 00:16:37,320
And I write it on a small paper, and I put it on the table.

381
00:16:38,540 --> 00:16:41,610
The first bird, on the table, ready for the morning.

382
00:16:42,470 --> 00:16:45,120
So in the morning, there is no cloud, Mia.

383
00:16:45,180 --> 00:16:46,090
There is one paper.

384
00:16:47,280 --> 00:16:49,440
I do the same with my notebook, from our first episode.

385
00:16:50,020 --> 00:16:50,510
One line.

386
00:16:51,280 --> 00:16:52,140
And I noticed something.

387
00:16:52,780 --> 00:16:57,020
When I know the first bird the night before, I sleep better.

388
00:16:57,120 --> 00:16:58,500
You sleep better, Ben.

389
00:16:58,520 --> 00:17:00,860
Because your head is not making a cloud all night.

390
00:17:01,340 --> 00:17:03,060
The cloud is on the paper.

391
00:17:03,120 --> 00:17:04,160
Your head is free.

392
00:17:05,320 --> 00:17:07,060
And do you have a third idea?

393
00:17:07,120 --> 00:17:09,290
Something for our listeners who are learning English?

394
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:11,220
Yes, Mia.

395
00:17:11,660 --> 00:17:13,940
Learning English is the biggest list of all.

396
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:15,680
Ten thousand words.

397
00:17:16,200 --> 00:17:16,900
All the grammar.

398
00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:19,980
Listening, speaking, reading, writing.

399
00:17:20,640 --> 00:17:22,520
It is not twenty-three things.

400
00:17:22,580 --> 00:17:24,560
It is twenty-three thousand things.

401
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:27,630
And people look at the whole thing and do nothing.

402
00:17:28,180 --> 00:17:29,800
For years.

403
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:31,670
What was your first bird, in Spanish?

404
00:17:33,160 --> 00:17:34,460
One podcast, Ben.

405
00:17:35,140 --> 00:17:37,560
One episode, every morning, with my tea.

406
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:39,080
That was the bird.

407
00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:43,510
Not "learn Spanish." Not "grammar." One episode.

408
00:17:44,080 --> 00:17:48,220
And I let the other twenty-three thousand things wait outside the door.

409
00:17:48,260 --> 00:17:51,700
And one day, months later, I understood the whole episode.

410
00:17:52,189 --> 00:17:53,840
The birds had become a report.

411
00:17:55,140 --> 00:17:58,920
"The birds had become a report." Mia, that is how it works.

412
00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:02,990
And for everyone listening now, this episode is the bird today.

413
00:18:03,820 --> 00:18:04,520
One episode.

414
00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:07,960
Nobody needs to learn all of English today.

415
00:18:08,080 --> 00:18:09,680
Just finish this one.

416
00:18:09,720 --> 00:18:11,220
And then, click.

417
00:18:11,280 --> 00:18:12,960
That click is English getting better.

418
00:18:14,210 --> 00:18:16,580
That click is English getting better.

419
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:17,500
I like that, Ben.

420
00:18:18,180 --> 00:18:19,950
And I want to say one honest thing.

421
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:23,140
One task at a time does not give more time.

422
00:18:23,710 --> 00:18:25,600
The day is still twenty-four hours.

423
00:18:26,120 --> 00:18:27,860
Twenty-three things may not fit.

424
00:18:28,440 --> 00:18:30,160
Some birds will wait until tomorrow.

425
00:18:30,660 --> 00:18:31,240
Is that okay?

426
00:18:32,080 --> 00:18:32,980
That is fine, Mia.

427
00:18:33,280 --> 00:18:34,860
The goal is not to finish everything.

428
00:18:35,349 --> 00:18:40,960
The goal is to finish something, every time, and to stop carrying half-finished things.

429
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:46,080
At the end of a one-task day, I have maybe eleven finished things and twelve waiting on paper.

430
00:18:46,740 --> 00:18:51,120
At the end of a cloud day, I have zero finished things and twenty-three crying.

431
00:18:51,740 --> 00:18:52,360
Same time.

432
00:18:52,820 --> 00:18:53,300
Different day.

433
00:18:54,370 --> 00:18:55,080
"Same time.

434
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:58,840
Different day." Ben, that is our message today, I think.

435
00:18:59,500 --> 00:19:01,600
A long list is not many tasks.

436
00:19:01,940 --> 00:19:02,840
It is a cloud.

437
00:19:03,320 --> 00:19:05,120
And nobody can start a cloud.

438
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:07,520
So we take one thing with edges.

439
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:08,720
We finish it.

440
00:19:09,320 --> 00:19:09,640
Click.

441
00:19:10,340 --> 00:19:11,200
Then the next one.

442
00:19:11,830 --> 00:19:15,320
The cloud goes away one bird at a time.

443
00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:16,540
One bird at a time.

444
00:19:17,440 --> 00:19:22,440
Mia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.

445
00:19:23,600 --> 00:19:23,860
One.

446
00:19:24,660 --> 00:19:26,460
Not two, not twenty-three.

447
00:19:27,160 --> 00:19:27,820
One task.

448
00:19:28,440 --> 00:19:29,140
One bird.

449
00:19:30,180 --> 00:19:30,580
Task.

450
00:19:31,120 --> 00:19:31,860
A piece of work.

451
00:19:32,250 --> 00:19:33,140
A small task.

452
00:19:34,120 --> 00:19:34,980
Finish the task.

453
00:19:36,260 --> 00:19:36,580
Time.

454
00:19:37,110 --> 00:19:38,200
Minutes and hours.

455
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:40,010
One task at a time.

456
00:19:40,620 --> 00:19:41,520
The time it needs.

457
00:19:42,920 --> 00:19:43,320
Next.

458
00:19:43,870 --> 00:19:45,100
The one after this one.

459
00:19:45,660 --> 00:19:46,600
What is the next one?

460
00:19:47,880 --> 00:19:48,220
Finish.

461
00:19:48,780 --> 00:19:49,420
To complete.

462
00:19:50,120 --> 00:19:51,160
Finish one thing.

463
00:19:51,860 --> 00:19:53,620
Finished things do not come back.

464
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:55,880
One.

465
00:19:55,980 --> 00:19:56,480
Task.

466
00:19:57,440 --> 00:19:57,850
Time.

467
00:19:59,000 --> 00:19:59,450
Next.

468
00:20:00,700 --> 00:20:01,070
Finish.

469
00:20:01,960 --> 00:20:04,240
Five words, one at a time.

470
00:20:04,270 --> 00:20:05,820
And one big idea.

471
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:07,520
Bird by bird.

472
00:20:08,660 --> 00:20:10,900
Now, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?

473
00:20:11,720 --> 00:20:12,660
Yes, Mia.

474
00:20:12,740 --> 00:20:14,660
Everyone listening, here is my question.

475
00:20:15,400 --> 00:20:17,040
What is your first bird tomorrow?

476
00:20:17,660 --> 00:20:18,340
Not the list.

477
00:20:18,730 --> 00:20:19,680
The first one.

478
00:20:20,480 --> 00:20:20,960
An email?

479
00:20:21,400 --> 00:20:21,900
A call?

480
00:20:22,260 --> 00:20:22,780
A page?

481
00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:25,520
Write it in the comments in one short sentence.

482
00:20:25,960 --> 00:20:33,040
For example, "My first bird tomorrow is one email to my boss." Reading those sentences is our bird, actually.

483
00:20:33,580 --> 00:20:35,800
We read them one at a time.

484
00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:37,000
One at a time.

485
00:20:37,700 --> 00:20:41,120
And if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.

486
00:20:41,580 --> 00:20:43,780
We make a new episode every few days.

487
00:20:44,260 --> 00:20:48,440
Slow English, simple words, and one true story each time.

488
00:20:49,140 --> 00:20:51,650
The full text of this episode is in the description.

489
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:53,700
One episode at a time.

490
00:20:54,170 --> 00:20:55,239
That is the plan.

491
00:20:56,120 --> 00:20:57,760
Thank you for spending this time with us.

492
00:20:58,360 --> 00:20:59,900
Maybe you have a long list today.

493
00:21:00,620 --> 00:21:01,760
Maybe it looks like a wall.

494
00:21:02,620 --> 00:21:03,940
Sit down for one minute.

495
00:21:04,430 --> 00:21:07,680
Ask, "What is the first one?" Do that one.

496
00:21:08,520 --> 00:21:09,160
Then the next.

497
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:11,430
Nobody needs to do everything.

498
00:21:12,280 --> 00:21:13,380
One thing.

499
00:21:13,420 --> 00:21:14,880
And then, one more.

500
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:16,500
This is Mia.

501
00:21:17,070 --> 00:21:17,959
And this is Ben.

502
00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:20,440
This was Two Cups English.

503
00:21:20,840 --> 00:21:21,980
Bird by bird.

504
00:21:22,560 --> 00:21:23,720
We will see you next time.

